from decimal import Decimal, InvalidOperation
def make_mess_report(filename):
    total = Decimal("0.00")
    report_rows = []
    rejected = []
    with open(filename, "r", encoding="utf-8") as file:
        for line_number, raw_line in enumerate(file, start=1):
            line = raw_line.strip()
            if not line:
                continue
            parts = [part.strip() for part in line.split(",")]
            if len(parts) != 3:
                rejected.append((line_number, raw_line.rstrip("\\n"),
                                 "expected 3 fields"))
                continue
            reg_no, name, amount_text = parts
            try:
                amount = Decimal(amount_text)
            except InvalidOperation:
                rejected.append((line_number, raw_line.rstrip("\\n"),
                                 "amount is not numeric"))
                continue
            if not amount.is_finite():
                rejected.append((line_number, raw_line.rstrip("\\n"),
                                 "amount is not finite"))
                continue
            total += amount
            report_rows.append(f"{name} {amount:.2f}")
    return "\\n".join(report_rows), total, rejected
if __name__ == "__main__":
    report, total, rejected = make_mess_report("mess_oct.txt")
    print(report)
    print("Total", f"{total:.2f}")
    print("Rejected lines:", rejected)
The function receives the file name as a parameter, so it is reusable with different months or folders. Decimal is used for
money to avoid ordinary binary floating-point rounding issues.
1.3 Design defence (under 100 words)
The repaired version uses a with statement, so the file is closed even if processing fails. It reads one line at a time,
keeping memory use nearly constant as the file grows. It validates the number of fields and parses amounts with
Decimal, so paise values are accepted. Specific validation failures are recorded with line numbers instead of being
silently discarded. A blank final line is safely ignored. Therefore, a 50,000-line file can be processed reliably without
holding the entire file or report in memory.
