3.4 Misuse experiment
Replacing the specific handlers with except Exception: pass and introducing a misspelled variable (for example, writing
presnt += 1 instead of present += 1) causes a NameError. Because NameError is an Exception subclass, the bare
handler catches it and silently ignores it. The program may finish with an incorrect count and no diagnostic. This is
dangerous because programming defects become indistinguishable from bad input.
Alternative concept and complexity
With exception-based validation, each valid line follows the normal parse path and exceptions are raised only for invalid
rows. When bad rows are rare, this is usually efficient and keeps validation code focused. With if-statements, each line is
checked explicitly before fields are used; this avoids exception overhead and is often preferable when malformed rows
are common. Both approaches are O(1) work per line for a fixed number of fields, so total processing is O(n) for n lines.
Their practical constant costs differ; benchmark with representative data to compare.
