• import attendance.stats imports the submodule; call its function as attendance.stats.percentage(...).
• from attendance import stats binds the stats submodule name directly; call stats.percentage(...).
• from attendance.stats import percentage imports the function name directly; call percentage(...). This is concise but
can make name collisions more likely.
Alternative concept and complexity
Keeping everything in one script avoids package setup and has no package import overhead, but locating and changing
a function becomes harder as the file grows. A package loads imported modules once per process and caches them in
sys.modules; subsequent imports normally reuse the cached module. Import work is generally a one-time startup cost.
Package memory includes the loaded module objects and their globals, usually modest compared with the benefits of
separation. Runtime algorithm complexity is unchanged by the organisation.
